## Background

When writing a CLI application on Node.js, there is a pain point when ask user for input. Without third-party module, currently the best way to ask user input is to use `readline` module.

However, it could be somehow "too complex" for some simple use case(like CLI-based [guessing number game](https://mdn.github.io/learning-area/javascript/introduction-to-js-1/first-splash/number-guessing-game.html) or just simply ask people to choose a project name in a script).
```js
const readline = require('readline');

const rl = readline.createInterface({
  input: process.stdin,
  output: process.stdout
});

rl.question('What do you think of Node.js? ', (answer) => {
  console.log(`Thank you for your valuable feedback: ${answer}`);
  rl.close();
});
```

In this PR, it adds a new global function `prompt`, which accepts a `message` and `defaultValue`. It echoes `message` to user, and returns user input synchronously. Example is shown as following

```
> prompt("hello world")
hello world
hello
'hello'
``` 

## API Design Consideration

The origin issue suggested something like `readline.input` or `process.input`. Thanks to @artembykov's suggestion, I think `prompt` on `global` would be ideal solution to given problem. `prompt` method is [standardzied](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#dom-prompt) in browser side, and also is implemented on `global` in [deno](https://doc.deno.land/builtin/stable#prompt). Introducing `prompt` will not make API in JS world scattering, while benifits user.

Fixes: https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/37925

